{0 Hydration and islands}

The server sends the first HTML of a page, so the page shows before any
script runs, and also with JavaScript off. The browser then starts the same
components and adopts that HTML instead of building it again. This is
hydration.

{1:server-html The HTML of the server}

The server mounts the app at the URL, runs the Remote calls of the first
render ([render_page_settled]), and prints the view. The [Doc_hello] component
calls the greeting endpoint when it starts. The page that the server of
{!page-server.try} sends has the answer in its HTML, and the table of answers
in a [script] element:

{@ocaml[
# let has =
    let html =
      (Doc_server.Server.handle Doc_server.server (Doc_server.request "GET" "/"))
        .Silica_server.body
    in
    fun text ->
      let n = String.length text in
      let rec at i =
        i + n <= String.length html && (String.sub html i n = text || at (i + 1))
      in
      at 0;;
val has : string -> bool = <fun>
# has "<p>Hello, Ada</p>", has {|id="silica-answers"|};;
- : bool * bool = (true, true)
]}

The rules of the server HTML:

- Two text nodes next to each other print with [<!-- -->] between them, so
  that the browser keeps them as two nodes.
- A portal prints nothing: popups open in the browser.
- The last [Set_title] is the title, and the last [Set_document_attribute] of
  each name is on [<html>], such as the [data-theme] of the theme (see
  {!page-theming}).
- The CSRF token is in [<meta name="silica-csrf">].

{1:answers Answers}

{!Silica.Answers} is the table of the Remote calls that the server answered
while it rendered the page: the path, the encoded input and the encoded
output. Before its first paint, the browser answers the same calls from this
table, and uses each answer once. So no call runs two times, and the first
view in the browser is the view of the server HTML. A call with no answer in
the table runs as usual. {!Silica.Answers.to_script} writes the table into
the page as JSON.

{@ocaml[
# let table =
    Silica.Answers.(add empty ~path:"/api/greeting" ~input:{|"Ada"|}
      ~output:{|"Hello, Ada"|});;
val table : Silica.Answers.t = <abstr>
# Silica.Answers.find table ~path:"/api/greeting" ~input:{|"Ada"|};;
- : string option = Some "\"Hello, Ada\""
]}

The server renders with no clock: timers, sources and Live subscriptions do
not run there. A call that fails, has no handler or takes longer than
[settle_timeout_ms] (1 second for the whole step) is left out, and the page
shows the state at that moment.

{1:adopt Adopt the HTML}

{!Silica_browser.mount} and {!Silica_browser.mount_app} hydrate by default.
The first paint walks the HTML in the root next to the view:

- It checks the tag, the type and the number of children of each node.
- It removes the [<!-- -->] markers between texts.
- It takes the value of the client for a text or an attribute that differs.
- It adds no handler to the nodes: events are delegated.
- A field whose text the user changed before the first paint keeps that text,
  and the machine gets it as an input event.

When the structure differs, the browser clears the root, renders again, and
logs the first difference to the console. In dev mode it raises an exception
instead, so that a test finds the difference. [~hydrate:false] clears the root
first.

After the first paint the root has the attribute [data-silica-ready]. From
then on the page reacts to input; browser tests wait for it.

The first props of the root must be the same in the server and in the
browser. A value that the page reads from a cookie, such as the theme, is read
on both sides before the mount: {!Silica_server.cookie} and
{!Silica_browser.cookie}.

{1:islands Islands}

An island is a place in a view where a Live component runs. Its machine runs
on the server, one instance for each connection. The client sends events and
gets each new state. Declare the component in shared code with its channel and
the codecs of its state and its events:

From [example/app/shared/example_counter.mlx]:
{@ocaml skip[
let live =
  Live.declare ~channel:"counter" ~state:Example_model.counter_state
    ~event:Example_model.counter_event component
]}

{!Silica.Live.slot} puts it in a view. It is an empty [div] with
[data-silica-live] set to the channel:

From [example/app/shared/example_home.mlx]:
{@ocaml skip[
    <section><h2>"Live counter"</h2> (Live.slot Example_counter.live)</section>
]}

The browser entry lists the Live components of the page:

From [example/client/client_main.ml]:
{@ocaml skip[
  mount_app ~root:"silica-root"
    ~lives:[ Live Example_counter.live ]
    Example_app.app
]}

And the server lists them in [islands] (see {!page-server.islands}). Then:

- The server render prints the view of the island at its initial state.
- In the browser, the island opens its socket when the element appears, and
  closes it when the element goes away. The browser never changes the
  children of the island element: the server HTML stays until the first state
  arrives, and that paint adopts it.
- While the socket is down, the island root has [data-silica-offline], and
  events are dropped, not queued. The client connects again after a delay.

{!Silica_browser.mount_live} mounts a Live component as the root of an
element, with no app around it.

A Live machine runs on the server, where there is no page. So it must not own
sources of the page (such as a focus trap or a key table) or use actions that
need an answer of the page (such as [Copy]). {!Check.live} reports both (see
{!page-checker}).
